<p>The World Health Organization said health authorities were responding to a cluster of hantavirus cases linked to the Dutch-flagged MV Hondius cruise ship in the Atlantic, with eight reported cases as of May 7, including three deaths and five laboratory-confirmed infections. WHO identified the virus as Andes virus, a hantavirus species associated with limited human-to-human transmission after close and prolonged contact, and said the public health risk was low while more cases could appear because the incubation period can be up to several weeks. The ship, operated by Oceanwide Expeditions, began its voyage from Ushuaia, Argentina, on April 1 and was expected to reach Spain&#x27;s Canary Islands on May 10, after three ill passengers were evacuated for treatment and countries began tracing passengers who had disembarked before the outbreak was detected. WHO said it was coordinating with countries under the International Health Regulations, deploying an expert to the vessel, sending 2,500 diagnostic kits from Argentina to laboratories in five countries, and preparing guidance for safe disembarkation and onward travel.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/A8cCQUaKcy1VOJulM2NnpktRhOY_5gII58ubZX5Qne4ekW-3xmj0JMlAyQRXCCsX2sPdy0WGtBFZaMa5XKuaCQQA-0mLwb-Jnde8MC84VrdQa9jtkHnmF7LXAKGTX29OfxWuYRClEflPa_ZpVplF2Yn7p89DCQc02-hDORVTrTD1h4CZwNyKFcO0f4BDeyIE2w' alt='An ambulance during an evacuation of suspected hantavirus patients in Praia, Cape Verde.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>U.S. monitoring: U.S. health officials were monitoring former MV Hondius passengers in at least five states, and The New York Times reported that people being monitored in Georgia, California and Arizona had shown no symptoms.</li><li>Cabo Verde stop: The MV Hondius was anchored off Praia, Cabo Verde, on May 5 before setting course for Spain on May 6.</li><li>Swiss case: Swiss authorities reported a confirmed passenger case in Zurich as evacuations and disembarkations continued.</li><li>Origin probe: Argentine investigators suspected the initial exposure may have occurred during a birdwatching trip in Ushuaia before boarding, two Health Ministry officials told the Associated Press.</li></ul><blockquote>While this is a serious incident, WHO assesses the public health risk as low. - Dr Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: WHO said the International Health Regulations exist to address cross-border health threats and require global cooperation and solidarity. (<a href='https://www.who.int/news/item/07-05-2026-who-s-response-to-hantavirus-cases-linked-to-a-cruise-ship'>WHO</a>)</li><li>Canary Islands government: Fernando Clavijo, president of the Canary Islands, said he favored carrying out the disembarkation operation in Cabo Verde. (<a href='https://www.nacion.com/el-mundo/asi-esta-avanzando-el-brote-de-hantavirus-que/XRG3PXFJEFEATDAULURKXBXH5U/story/'>La Nación</a>)</li><li>Oceanwide Expeditions: The operator said it had contacted all passengers who already disembarked at St Helena, where 30 passengers left the vessel on April 24. (<a href='https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/health/how-worried-should-we-be-about-hantavirus.html'>Graphic Online</a>)</li><li>U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention: The CDC said U.S. officials were closely monitoring the situation, and Live Science reported the agency was preparing official guidance. (<a href='https://www.livescience.com/health/live/hantivirus-cruise-thursday-may-7'>Live Science</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/hantavirus-cruise-ship-mv-hondius-passengers-monitored-us-worldwide/'>5 states monitoring passengers who departed ship stricken by hantavirus</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/les-decodeurs/article/2026/05/08/from-the-andes-strain-to-the-fatality-rate-a-glossary-of-terms-for-understanding-hantaviruses_6753238_8.html'>A glossary to understand the terms circulating with h